 The radius of the sphere, $r$, holds a key information on the length of the line segment from the center of the sphere, the point where the hemisphere touches the ground. Notice that the largest cube that fits inside a sphere will have a diagonal equal to the diameter of the sphere. The diagonal of the cube can be found using the cube's radius, $s$: $2 \sqrt[3]{s^2}$. So we have \begin{align*}
2r = 2 \sqrt[3]{s^2}.
\end{align*} Therefore the cube's side is \begin{align*}
s = r \sqrt[3]{4}.
\end{align*} For the problem at hand, the length of the radius of the hemisphere ($s$) to the radius of the sphere ($r$) is \begin{align*}
a &= \frac{s}{r} = \sqrt[3]{4}.
\end{align*} Therefore the value of $a$ is $\boxed{4}$. </think> <answer>$\boxed{4}$</answer>

